From 6db74c35ecf55ce4834a9b80fbe28e13b88fc588 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 17:38:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] Keep a pressed key highlighted for a minimum time

The pressed-key highlight was invisible on the iPAQ h2200. A protocol log
of a real session shows why: a tap lasts 35 to 76 ms between button press
and release, the highlighted redraw is committed about 19 ms after the
press and the plain one about 15 ms after the release, so the highlight is
on screen for roughly 60 ms. That is too short for a panel with a slow
response and an upload per frame. On top of that the ipaq colours changed
a key only from grey 45 to grey 100.

Two changes:

The ipaq 'high' colour of both schemes is now a strong opaque blue
(RGB 60,140,230). It is 4.0:1 against the key grey 45 (2.3:1 before) and
4.7:1 against the darker function keys. The white pressed text on it is
3.5:1, lower than on the old grey (5.9:1) but readable at the 13 px
bitmap font; the key shape is what has to be seen.

--highlight-ms MS (0 to 1000, 0 = off, as before) keeps the key drawn
highlighted until MS after its press even when the button is released
earlier. Only the redraw of the plain key waits; the virtual keyboard
release still goes out at the true time, so typing is not delayed. A new
press flushes the pending redraw first. A layer redraw, a resize and hide
also drop it: a layer redraw repaints the held key itself, and hide
draws it plain before the surface is unmapped, so nothing stale is shown
when it is mapped again. The deadline is added to the main loop's poll
timeout, next to the label cache wait, and checked after the Wayland
events of the same wakeup so a press cancels it. The default comes from
DEFAULT_HIGHLIGHT_MS in the layout config: 150 for ipaq, 0 elsewhere.

The schedule is a Wayland-free unit (hold.c) that only sees the
monotonic milliseconds it is given, with its own tests.
